{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments ["<b>And Canaan shall be a servant to them</b>. Rabbi Abraham (Ibn Ezra on Genesis 9:26) explained that \"to them\" (לָמוֹ) means that Canaan shall be a servant to Elohim and to Shem, for He will compel him to serve the El. And in a second interpretation he explained that he shall be a servant to Japheth and to Shem. If so, Noah came to curse his enemies — and behold, he blessed them: that Canaan shall be a servant to Elohim. And Rashi wrote (Rashi on Genesis 9:26): \"Blessed be YHWH, the Elohim of Shem\" — who is destined to keep His promise to the seed of Shem, to give them the land of Canaan, and Canaan shall be to them a subjected servant. And He reversed and said again, so to speak, that even when the children of Shem go into exile, they shall buy servants to themselves from the children of Canaan.\n\nAnd the correct interpretation in my eyes is that at first he cursed him that he shall be a servant of servants to the entire world, and everyone who found him would enslave him. For the meaning of \"to his brothers\" (לְאֶחָיו) is to all the children of men, in the manner of \"I will send all the men, each to his neighbor\" (זכריה ח י) — like the meaning of \"man by man\" (ישעיהו ג ה). Or \"to his brothers\" means to Shem and Japheth, for the brothers of one's father are called his brothers, as in the manner of \"for his brother was taken captive\" (בראשית י\"ד:י\"ד) — for Abraham called Lot his brother, since Lot was the son of Abraham's brother. And some say (Ibn Ezra and Radak) \"to his brothers\" means the sons of his father, for once he is a servant to the sons of his father — to Shem and to Japheth — behold, he is a servant to the entire world.\n\nAnd He blessed first, \"the Elohim of Shem\" — He made it known to him that Shem shall be a servant of Elohim, and Canaan shall be a servant to him; and \"to them\" (לָמוֹ) alludes to the seed of Shem, who are many. And it is possible that \"to them\" refers back also to \"his brothers\" mentioned above.\n\nAnd He turned back and blessed Japheth with the enlargement of territory, and Shem with the dwelling of Elohim in his tents, and that Canaan shall be a servant to them — to both of them. And behold, He enslaved Canaan to Shem twice, a hint that he shall inherit his land and all that is his, for whatever a servant acquires, his master acquires (פסחים פח). And this passage was written to inform us that through his sin Canaan became an eternal servant, and Abraham merited his land. And the matter of the wine was written concerning Noah, for it contains a warning against it more than the passage of the nazirite, for the perfectly righteous man whose righteousness saved the entire world — even him the wine caused to sin, and brought him to disgrace and to the curse of his seed."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:26:54.969138413Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}